<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nikola Tesla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1856">1856</a>–<a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Tesla</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nikola Tesla was a Serbian-American engineer, futurist and inventor known for his contributions to the design of the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alternating-current">alternating current</a> (AC) electricity supply system. Born in Smiljan in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>, he migrated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1884 and worked briefly at the Edison Machine Works before setting up his own laboratories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. His AC induction motor and polyphase patents, licensed by Westinghouse Electric in 1888, became the basis of the polyphase system. His Wardenclyffe Tower project for wireless communication and power ran out of funding. He died in New York City in 1943; in 1960 the SI unit of magnetic flux density was named the tesla.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nikola-tesla.jpg" alt="Nikola Tesla" width="330" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Napoleon Sarony · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tesla_circa_1890.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The progressive development of man is vitally dependent on invention. It is the most important product of his creative brain. Its ultimate purpose is the complete mastery of mind over the material world, the harnessing of the forces of nature to human needs.&#8221;</p><footer>My Inventions (Electrical Experimenter, 1919) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/My_Inventions" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Then I observed to my delight that I could visualize with the greatest facility. I needed no models, drawings or experiments. I could picture them all as real in my mind.&#8221;</p><footer>My Inventions (Electrical Experimenter, 1919) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/My_Inventions" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;War can not be avoided until the physical cause for its recurrence is removed and this, in the last analysis, is the vast extent of the planet on which we live.&#8221;</p><footer>My Inventions (Electrical Experimenter, 1919) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/My_Inventions" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
